[FINAL-TEXT] **Root cause found — direct evidence confirmed.**

Router6's `nft list ruleset` shows a FORWARD chain ACL:

```
chain FORWARD {
    type filter hook forward priority filter; policy accept;
    iifname "eth2" ip saddr 172.16.10.0/24 ip daddr 172.18.51.1 tcp dport 80 ... accept   ← narrow permit
    iifname "eth2" meta l4proto tcp counter packets 0 bytes 0 drop                          ← blocks TCP
    iifname "eth2" meta l4proto udp counter packets 0 bytes 0 drop                          ← blocks UDP
    iifname "eth2" counter packets 32 bytes 2688 drop                                       ← blocks ALL else (ICMP etc.)
}
```

The **last rule has 32 matched packets / 2688 bytes** — that is live ICMP being silently dropped. `eth2` is router6's uplink to router3 (the r6_r3 link). Any packet entering from the WAN side (router3/wider network) that needs to be forwarded into 172.18.51.0/24 is dropped. This is exactly why:
- Router4 can ping router6's **own** interface 172.18.51.254 (INPUT chain, not FORWARD) ✅  
- Router6 can reach PC17 itself (OUTPUT chain, not FORWARD) ✅  
- All cross-router transit through router6 fails ❌

=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router6
diagnosis_text:
**Affected component:** The nftables `FORWARD` chain on router6, specifically filtering on ingress interface `eth2` (the r6_r3 uplink to router3 and the rest of the routing domain).

**Mechanism:** Router6 has four rules in its FORWARD hook: a narrow permit for TCP/80 from 172.16.10.0/24 to 172.18.51.1, then blanket DROP rules for all TCP, all UDP, and all remaining protocols (ICMP, etc.) entering via `eth2`. Any transit packet arriving from the wider network on eth2 and destined for the 172.18.51.0/24 LAN (or vice versa in the return direction) is silently discarded. The catch-all rule has 32 matched packets (2688 bytes) at time of inspection, confirming active dropping. Because the rules apply only to the FORWARD hook, router6's own IP responses (INPUT/OUTPUT) remain functional — which is why router4 can ping 172.18.51.254 (router6's own interface) but not PC17 (172.18.51.17), and why router6 itself can ping PC17.

**Localization:** router6, FORWARD chain, ingress filter on interface `eth2`. The fault blocks all inbound-transit traffic from the routing core into the 172.18.51.0/24 subnet, effectively isolating PC15–PC18 from any off-subnet communication and preventing PC11–PC14 from reaching the 172.18.51.0/24 segment. (PC11–PC14 can still reach 172.16.10.0/24 via a path that bypasses router6 entirely.)

**Root cause:** An over-broad ACL on router6's FORWARD chain drops all traffic entering the router via its WAN-facing interface (eth2), with the sole exception of HTTP from 172.16.10.0/24 to one specific host.

**Fix:** Remove or revise the three DROP rules on `iifname "eth2"` in router6's nftables FORWARD chain (e.g., `nft delete rule ip filter FORWARD handle <N>`) so that legitimate transit traffic is permitted between the 172.18.51.0/24 LAN and the wider network.
=== END ===